  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1386",
      "html": "<p>Common name: Niger Oto. Type locality: Rio das Pedras of Rio Ribeira, Brazil. Size: 6 cm SL.</p><p>A non-descript species with an attractive if low key horizontally striped and mottled patterning. Lives sympatrically with Parotocinclus maculicauda.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1386",
      "html": "<p>South America: Ribeira de Iguape River basin, rivers of São Paulo State, Brazil.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1386",
      "html": "<p>pH 6.4–7.4. Temperature 20–24°C. Prefers slightly cooler water.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "equipment",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1386",
      "html": "<p>Really does best in a planted aquarium, other cover is not necessary although the fish has at least a passing affinity with rounded pebbles.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1386",
      "html": "<p>These fish appear to be predominantly herbivorous and will readily feed from a wide array of aquarium fare. Algae discs can make up a good portion of their diet, supplemented with any number of additional prepared flake or pellet foods.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1386",
      "html": "<p>Peaceful. Suggested tankmates: most small community fishes that prefer a little current and temperatures on the lower side.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1386",
      "html": "<p>Females are larger, broader across the pectoral to dorsal area and slightly duller in colouration.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1386",
      "html": "<p>These fish are egg scatterers, breeding in a fashion much like a member of the callichthyid genus Corydoras.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1386",
      "html": "<p>Kosmos, Rio de Janeiro [Rev. Art. Sci. Litt.] v. 5 (no. 2) (for 1907), pp [2].</p>"
    }
  ],
